 "raw_reply": "{\n  \"experiment_id\": \"EXP-019\",\n  \"observed_evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The selection rule is mean ROC-AUC minus 0.25×fold standard deviation, then runtime, using training CV only.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-019 / evidence.selection_rule\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"extra_trees has mean ROC-AUC 0.8978 and std 0.0244 over 3 folds, with 1 repeat, default params and the raw stage.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-019 / evidence.model_results[1].metrics.roc_auc (mean, std); evidence.model_results[1].folds, .repeats, .model_params, .stage\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"logistic_regression has mean ROC-AUC 0.8941 and std 0.0198, so it is numerically close to extra_trees and has the lower fold std.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-019 / evidence.model_results[0].metrics.roc_auc (mean, std)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The ROC-AUC 95% CIs of extra_trees (0.8702 to 0.9254) and logistic_regression (0.8717 to 0.9165) overlap almost completely.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-019 / evidence.model_results[1].metrics.roc_auc (ci95_low, ci95_high); evidence.model_results[0].metrics.roc_auc (ci95_low, ci95_high)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"Per-fold ROC-AUC, extra_trees vs logistic_regression: fold 1 is 0.8993 vs 0.8771, fold 2 is 0.9214 vs 0.9158, fold 3 is 0.8727 vs 0.8894. extra_trees wins two folds and loses one.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-019 / evidence.model_results[1].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc; evidence.model_results[0].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"On secondary metrics, logistic_regression has higher mean accuracy (0.8140 vs 0.7974), higher F1 (0.7905 vs 0.7766) and lower ECE (0.0979 vs 0.1095). Mean log-loss (0.4025 vs 0.4010) and Brier (0.1297 vs 0.1291) are essentially tied.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-019 / evidence.model_results[0].metrics and evidence.model_results[1].metrics (accuracy, f1, ece_10, log_loss, brier)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"The other families have lower mean ROC-AUC: hist_gradient_boosting 0.8620, lightgbm 0.8700, xgboost 0.8574.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-019 / evidence.model_results[2].metrics.roc_auc.mean; evidence.model_results[3].metrics.roc_auc.mean; evidence.model_results[4].metrics.roc_auc.mean\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"All metrics use a fixed 0.5 decision threshold and every model has optimization 'baseline' with empty model_params. The limitations list in the bundle is empty.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-019 / evidence.model_results[*].fold_metrics[*].threshold; evidence.model_results[*].optimization; evidence.model_results[*].model_params; limitations\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"statement\": \"extra_trees feature importance is concentrated in ca, thal, cp, exang and thalach, with top10_frequency of 1.0 for the top 10 features. sex, restecg and fbs fall outside the top 10 in all folds.\",\n      \"citation\": \"EXP-019 / evidence.model_results[1].feature_stability\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"interpretation\": \"Inference (derived from the cited numbers, not an observed field): applying the stated rule gives extra_trees about 0.8917 and logistic_regression about 0.8892. extra_trees therefore ranks first by a margin of about 0.0025, far smaller than either model's fold std (about 0.02). Fold-level differences change sign, and the CIs overlap. So the evidence supports 'extra_trees is the nominal training-CV leader under this rule'. It does not support 'strongest stable' or a meaningful separation from logistic_regression, which is the more stable and better-calibrated family on these folds. The boosting families trail by roughly 0.02 to 0.04 ROC-AUC at default settings, but with 3 folds and no tuning even that gap is weakly established. The claim's stated limitation (CV leader is not a universal winner, locked holdout unseen) is appropriate but understates the problem: the leader is statistically indistinguishable from a simple baseline. Feature importances are predictive associations only and carry no causal meaning.\",\n  \"decision\": \"Retain extra_trees only as a provisional training-CV candidate, tied with logistic_regression pending a paired, repeated-CV comparison. Do not describe it as the winner, do not touch the locked holdout on the basis of this evidence alone, and do not approve any feature or model for deployment.\",\n  \"risks_and_missing_evidence\": [\n    \"Only 3 folds with 1 repeat on a small dataset (about 80 test rows per fold, inferred from the accuracy values). The fold std estimate is itself very noisy, so the 0.25×std penalty is unreliable.\",\n    \"No paired fold-difference analysis or corrected resampled test between extra_trees and logistic_regression. Per-fold differences are +0.022, +0.006 and -0.017.\",\n    \"All models run at default hyperparameters (model_params is empty, optimization 'baseline'). The result compares defaults, not algorithm families, and tuned boosting could change the ranking. Any tuning must be nested to avoid selection optimism.\",\n    \"Selection among 5 candidates on the same folds introduces optimistic selection bias. The reported 0.8978 is not an unbiased performance estimate.\",\n    \"Preprocessing is not documented (imputation, encoding of categorical-coded features such as cp, thal, slope, restecg, scaling for logistic_regression). It is unclear whether this is fit inside folds. Stratification, shuffling and seeds are unspecified.\",\n    \"No evidence on duplicate rows or patient grouping. Duplicates or repeated patients across folds would inflate CV scores. Group-aware splitting has not been confirmed.\",\n    \"Decision-time and production-availability review is missing for features. ca (fluoroscopy vessel count) and thal (stress-test result) are diagnostic test outputs that may be unavailable at the intended prediction moment or may be downstream of the diagnosis process. They are the top-ranked extra_trees features, so a leakage or availability risk could drive the headline score.\",\n    \"The target definition and prediction timepoint (which decision this model supports) are not stated, so it is impossible to judge feature legitimacy.\",\n    \"Importance stability is reported from one importance type per model. Impurity-style importances are biased toward high-cardinality or continuous features, are not comparable across model types, and cannot be read causally. hist_gradient_boosting has an empty feature_stability list.\",\n    \"Metrics are computed at a fixed 0.5 threshold. extra_trees ECE is about 0.11 and calibration is unaddressed, so probabilistic use is unsupported. ROC-AUC alone does not establish operating-point utility.\",\n    \"Only 5 algorithm families were tested (no random forest, SVM, kNN or regularized variants), so 'strongest family' is limited to this candidate set.\",\n    \"The bundle's limitations field is empty even though the claim lists one.\"\n  ],\n  \"challenges_to_claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-019-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The ROC-AUC margin over logistic_regression (mean 0.8978 vs 0.8941; rule score about 0.8917 vs 0.8892) is much smaller than fold variability. CIs overlap and per-fold wins are mixed (2 of 3). The claim presents extra_trees as the candidate without noting it is effectively tied with the baseline, which has lower std, higher accuracy and better ECE.\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-019-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The question asks about 'stable' evidence, but extra_trees has a higher fold std (0.0244) than logistic_regression (0.0198), and the stability penalty weight of 0.25 is arbitrary. A different weight or a different metric such as accuracy or ECE could reorder the top two.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-019-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"Default-parameter baselines with 3 folds and 1 repeat do not support conclusions about algorithm families. No nested tuning or repeated CV is present, and the selection-bias effect of ranking 5 candidates is unquantified.\",\n      \"severity\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"EXP-019-C1\",\n      \"issue\": \"The 'raw features' basis has no decision-time or production-availability review. Top features such as ca and thal may be diagnostic outputs, so the score cannot be tied to a deployable prediction setting. Feature importances must not be read causally.\",\n      \"severity\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"next_experiment\": {\n    \"title\": \"Paired repeated-CV head-to-head: extra_trees vs logistic_regression, with a feature-availability audit\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"If extra_trees truly has higher ranking ability than logistic_regression on training data, its paired ROC-AUC advantage will remain positive under repeated stratified CV (e.g. 5 folds × 10 repeats, identical splits and in-fold preprocessing). If the 3-fold gap was noise, the paired difference will include zero. This is a predictive hypothesis only.\",\n    \"success_gate\": \"Pre-registered: extra_trees is retained as leader only if the mean paired ROC-AUC difference (extra_trees minus logistic_regression) is >= +0.01, the 95% CI from a corrected resampled t-test (or paired bootstrap over repeats) excludes 0, and extra_trees wins in >= 70% of fold-repeat pairs. Otherwise the hypothesis is rejected, the two are declared tied, and logistic_regression is preferred for simplicity and calibration. In parallel, a documented decision-time availability check of ca, thal, cp, exang, oldpeak and slope against the intended prediction moment must be completed. Any feature failing the check is removed and the comparison re-run.\",\n    \"why_smallest\": \"It reruns only the two top-ranked models on the existing training data with the same pipeline and changes only the resampling scheme (more folds and repeats, paired analysis). It needs no new models, no tuning and no holdout access, and it directly tests whether the 0.0037 mean gap is distinguishable from noise.\"\n  },\n  \"overall_confidence\": \"low\"\n}",
